    "---\n",
    "title: \"The Bohr Model -- hydrogen-like atoms, effective Hamiltonian, and spectral series\"\n",
    "format: pdf\n",
    "editor: visual\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Purpose\n",
    "\n",
    "In this lab you will build an **effective Hamiltonian** for hydrogen-like atoms using the Bohr energy levels and use Python/QuTiP to compute and visualize **energy spectra** and **spectral series**. You will:\n",
    "\n",
    "  - Compute the Bohr energy $E_n \\propto -Z^2/n^2$ and represent $\\{|n\\rangle\\}_{n=1}^{n_{\\max}}$ as a finite Hilbert space.\n",
    "  \n",
    "  - Construct the effective Hamiltonian\n",
    "  \n",
    "  $$ \\hat{H}_{\\text{eff}}=\\sum_{n=1}^{n_{\\max}}E_n|n\\rangle\\langle n|$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and verify that diagonalizaiton produces the expected $E_n$.\n",
    "\n",
    "  - Predict emission absorption wavelengths using the **Rydberg formula** and generate **stick spectra** for Lyman, Balmer, Paschen, Brackett, and Pfund series.\n",
    "  \n",
    "  - Explore how spectra shift with nuclear charge $Z$ (hydrogenic ions such as He⁺, Li^{2+},...).\n",
    "  \n",
    "  - **Finite nuclear mass (reduced mass):** compare $R_{\\infty}$ vs the corrected $R\\approx R_{\\infty} (\\mu/m_e)$ and quantify the wavelength shift.\n",
    "  \n",
    "  - **Toy relaxation model (open system):** implement effective decays $|n\\rangle \\rightarrow |n-1\\rangle$ with collapse operators and visualize population relaxation (effective model; not a full QED derivation).\n",
    "  \n",
    "**QED (Quantum Electrodynamics).** QED is the quantum field theory that describes how charged matter (e.g., electrons) interacts with the electromagnetic field by *quantizing* the field (light appears as photons). It provides first-principles predicitons for processes such as absorption/emission of radiation, scattering, and radiative corrections in atoms. In this lab we use an **efective** open-system (Lindblad) relaxation model rather than a full QED derivation of spontaneous emission.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## Methods and material\n",
    "\n",
    "  - Computer with **Python 3** and **JupyterLab** (or Jupyter Notebook).\n",
    "  \n",
    "  - Packages: **numpy, matplotlib, qutip** (optional: **scipy, pandas**).\n",
    "  \n",
    "  - This is a **computational lab** (no physical spectroscopy setup is used).\n",
    "  \n",
    "\n",
    "## Step 0 -- Imports and constants\n",
    "\n",
    "**Theory**\n",
    "\n",
    "We will use the SI units for dynamics (J, s, m) and eV for reporting energies. Use:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\hbar = \\frac{h}{2\\pi}, \\,\\ 1\\text{eV} = e \\text{J}.$$"
   ]
  },
